.docname {Digital Twin Service}
.include {docs}

.glossary
    | Word | Meaning | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | Twin | The stored shape of a building: rooms with position, size, capacity | `HQ`, 12 rooms |
    | Upload | One `POST /register`, tracked by a handle until `ready` or `failed` | handle `b1` |
    | Handle | The id the caller polls; it is the new building's own id | `5c1e0b7a-…` |
    | Job | The stored upload waiting for the worker, in `pending_uploads` | `status: pending` |
    | Lease | A time during which one worker owns a job; when it runs out, the job can be taken again | 30 s |
    | Worker | A loop inside the service that takes the next job and provisions it | `worker.rs` |
    | Provision | Store the twin, announce it to `telemetry`, ask `dashboard` for starting columns | |
    | Placement | Where one sensor sits in the building: `x`, `y`, `z` | router at (4, 0.5, 6) |
    | Port | A Rust trait the use cases need, in their own words | `BuildingStore` |
    | Adapter | The code that does a port's job with a real technology | `MongoBuildings` |
    | Cedar | The policy language the access rules are written in | `Read`, `Edit` |
    | Cell | A 0.5 m square of floor, used to estimate where a phone is | (13.75, 6.25) |
    | Track | One phone followed from tick to tick | token `7f3c…` |
    | Dot | One estimated person sent to the browser | Lobby, floor 0 |

Implements the [Digital Twin](../architecture/twin-architecture.qd) architecture. Rust, Axum, MongoDB, Kafka, Redis. Route `/twin/*`, checked at the edge.

Five jobs:

| Job | Entry |
|---|---|
| Register a building | `POST /register`, the worker, the Kafka answer |
| Read and change buildings | `GET`/`PATCH /building/{id}`, lists, counts |
| Tell other services who owns a building | `GET /domain/{building}` |
| Store where sensors sit | `GET`/`PUT /building/{id}/placements` |
| Estimate where phones are | Kafka `wifi-observations` |

---

## Layout

Ports and adapters. Arrows point inward: only `main.rs` knows the store is MongoDB.

| Path | Holds | May not import |
|---|---|---|
| `src/domain/` | `Building`, `Room`, `Coordinates`, `Dimensions`, input checks, `Placement`, `PlacementChanges`, `AcceptedUpload`, `UploadStatus`, `GatewayClaims`, `DomainError` | `axum`, `mongodb`, `crate::adapters`, `crate::service` |
| `src/service/` | Use cases `buildings`, `provisioning`, `placements`, `tracking`, `tracks`, `estimator`; `authz` (Cedar); `ports.rs`; `fakes.rs` (test doubles) | `axum`, `mongodb`, `crate::adapters` |
| `src/adapters/driving/` | `http_api/` (handlers, claims extractor, error mapping), `worker.rs`, `kafka_consumer.rs`, `wifi_consumer.rs` | `crate::adapters::driven` |
| `src/adapters/driven/` | `persistence/` (`db.rs`, `jobs.rs`, `placements.rs`), `kafka_producer.rs`, `redis_devices.rs`, `outbound.rs` | |
| `src/adapters/` | `metrics.rs`, `ratelimit.rs`, `topics.rs`: shared by every route | |
| `src/lib.rs` | `build_router`, and `/contracts` | |
| `src/main.rs` | Reads env, builds adapters, starts everything | |

`tests/architecture_fitness.rs` reads every file under each folder and fails when one names a forbidden import.

| Port (`service/ports.rs`) | Methods | Adapter |
|---|---|---|
| `BuildingStore` | `find_by_id`, `find_by_domain`, `find_by_name`, `upsert`, `delete`, `counts_by_domain` | `MongoBuildings` |
| `UploadQueue` | `enqueue`, `claim`, `mark_ready`, `mark_failed`, `status` | `MongoUploadQueue` |
| `PlacementStore` | `load`, `apply` | `MongoPlacements` |
| `RegistrationEvents` | `publish_building_registration_request` | `KafkaEventProducer` |
| `OccupancyEvents` | `publish_occupancy` | `KafkaEventProducer` |
| `DevicesFeed` | `publish` | `RedisDevicesFeed` |
| `DownstreamSync` | `clone_thresholds`, `init_preferences`, `notify_provisioning_failed` | `OutboundConfig` |

Ports return `anyhow::Result`. A use case turns that into `DomainError::Internal`, which the HTTP layer answers as `500`.

---

## Start-up

`main.rs`, in order:

1. Connect MongoDB, database `crowdvision`, collection `buildings`; create its indexes.
2. Build `OutboundConfig` with one HTTP client, timeout 5 s.
3. Build `KafkaEventProducer`: first make sure topics `building-registration-requested`, `building-registration-completed` and `estimated-occupancy` exist.
4. Open `placements` and `pending_uploads`; create their indexes.
5. Start the **worker** loop.
6. Start the **registration consumer** (`building-registration-completed`).
7. Build `RedisDevicesFeed` (connects only on first publish) and start the **Wi-Fi consumer**.
8. Build the router with the rate limiter on, and serve on `PORT` until SIGTERM or Ctrl+C.

A failure in steps 1 to 4 stops the process: the service never runs half wired.

| Variable | Default | Used for |
|---|---|---|
| `MONGO_URI` | `mongodb://localhost:27017` | All three collections |
| `KAFKA_BROKERS` | `localhost:9092` | Registration, Wi-Fi ticks, room counts |
| `REDIS_URL` | `redis://localhost:6379` | Dots on `twin:devices:{id}` |
| `TELEMETRY_URL` | `http://localhost:3000` | Room limits after an edit |
| `DASHBOARD_URL` | `http://localhost:3001` | Starting columns |
| `NOTIFICATION_URL` | `http://localhost:3004` | "Provisioning failed" alert |
| `NODE_ENV` | | `test` turns off the three HTTP calls above |
| `PORT` | `3000` | HTTP |

---

## What it stores

MongoDB, database `crowdvision`:

| Collection | One document per | Shape | Indexes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `buildings` | building | `{id, name, rooms: [{id, name, capacity, position, dimensions, color?}], domains}` | `id` unique; `domains` |
| `pending_uploads` | upload | the building, the caller's raw claims, `status`, `attempts`, `leased_until`, `accepted_at`, `error`, `finished_at` | `id` unique; `status` + `leased_until`; `finished_at` with expiry 1 h |
| `placements` | building | `{_id: buildingId, sensors: {"<sensorId>": {x, y, z}}}` | `_id` |

- A finished upload is kept 1 h, then MongoDB removes it. The browser polls status for up to 30 s, so the record must outlive that.
- At start-up, finished uploads with no `finished_at` get one, or the expiry would never remove them.
- Tracks and dots live **in memory only**. A restart starts every building from no tracks.

---

## Routes

The edge strips `/twin`. Every route except the public ones needs `x-gateway-claims`.

| Method | Path | Who may call | Does | Answers |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `POST` | `/register` | Editor (`business_staff` or higher) in **every** listed domain | Check the building, queue it | `202 {buildingId}` |
| `GET` | `/building/{id}/status` | Any signed-in caller | Upload status | `{status: pending\|ready\|failed}` |
| `GET` | `/building/{id}` | Member of one of its domains, or `system:ap-simulator` | One building | `Building` |
| `PATCH` | `/building/{id}` | Editor in one of its **current** domains | Change `name`, `domains`; send room limits to `telemetry` | `Building` |
| `POST` | `/building/{id}/sync` | Editor in one of its domains | Announce it to `telemetry` again | `202` |
| `GET` | `/buildings/{domain}` | Member of that domain | All its buildings | `[Building]` |
| `POST` | `/buildings/counts` | Any signed-in caller | Count per domain, unreadable domains dropped | `{counts: {domain: n}}` |
| `GET` | `/domain/{building}` | Any signed-in caller | Domains owning the building, by id then by name | `["eng"]` |
| `GET` | `/building/{id}/placements` | Member, or `system:ap-simulator` | Sensor positions, sorted by id | `[{sensorId, position}]` |
| `PUT` | `/building/{id}/placements` | Editor in one of its domains | Add, move, remove positions in one batch | `204` |
| `GET` | `/contracts` | Public | Metric catalog: `roomName`, `roomMaxOccupancy` | `ServiceMetricsContract` |
| `GET` | `/health`, `/metrics` | Public | Liveness, Prometheus | |

---

## One request, layer by layer

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant E as Edge
        participant RL as ratelimit::rate_limit
        participant M as metrics::track_metrics
        participant X as GatewayClaims extractor
        participant H as handler
        participant U as use case
        participant Er as DomainError into_response
        E->>M: request
        M->>RL: request
        RL->>RL: client_ip: last x-forwarded-for entry, else peer
        RL->>RL: count for this IP in a 60 s window
        alt over 300
            RL-->>E: 429
        else
            RL->>X: request
            X->>X: header present, base64, JSON, has a user id?
            X->>H: GatewayClaims
            H->>H: wire input into domain types
            H->>U: call
            U-->>H: Ok or DomainError
            H-->>Er: on error
            Er-->>E: status and {type, message}
        end

| `DomainError` | Status | `type` |
|---|---|---|
| `Validation` | 400 | `Validation Error` |
| `Unauthorized` | 401 | `Unauthorized Error`: missing or unreadable header, no user id |
| `Forbidden` | 403 | `Forbidden Error` |
| `NotFound` | 404 | `Not Found Error` |
| `Internal` | 500 | `Internal Server Error`, generic text; the cause is only logged |

**Rate limit.** 300 requests per IP per 60 s, counted in memory. The IP is the **last** `x-forwarded-for` entry, the one the edge added, so a client writing its own value does not get a fresh counter. Idle IPs are dropped once per window.

---

## Registering a building

### Upload, call by call

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant B as Browser
        participant H as add_building
        participant P as Provisioning
        participant Q as MongoUploadQueue
        participant W as worker.rs
        participant S as MongoBuildings
        participant K as KafkaEventProducer
        participant D as OutboundConfig
        B->>H: POST /register {name, rooms, domains}
        H->>H: RoomWireInput::into_room for each room
        H->>H: new UUID, normalize_building_name
        H->>P: accept(building, claims)
        P->>P: domains not empty, can_edit_every(claims, domains)
        P->>Q: enqueue: status pending, attempts 0
        H-->>B: 202 {buildingId}
        W->>P: provision_next(30 s)
        P->>Q: claim: a pending job, lease free or run out
        Q-->>P: AcceptedUpload, lease until now + 30 s, attempts + 1
        P->>S: upsert(building)
        P->>K: publish_building_registration_request
        P->>D: init_preferences: POST dashboard /preferences/init/{id}
        B->>H: GET /building/{id}/status, every few seconds
        H-->>B: pending

### Data at each step

Example: one room, editor in `eng`.

| Step | Data |
|---|---|
| 1 | `{"name":" HQ ","domains":["eng"],"rooms":[{"id":"r1","name":"","capacity":20,"position":{"x":5,"y":1.5,"z":4},"dimensions":{"width":10,"height":3,"depth":8}}]}` |
| 2 | `Room {id: r1, name: r1, capacity: 20.0, …}`: blank name becomes the id |
| 3 | `Building {id: 5c1e…, name: HQ, domains: [eng]}`: name trimmed |
| 6 | `pending_uploads`: `{id: 5c1e…, building, claims: "<raw header>", status: pending, attempts: 0}` |
| 7 | `202 {"buildingId":"5c1e…"}` |
| 10 | same job, `leased_until` now + 30 s, `attempts` 1 |
| 12 | `{"buildingId":"5c1e…","name":"HQ","rooms":[{"id":"r1","name":"r1"}]}` on Kafka, key `5c1e…` |
| 13 | `POST …/preferences/init/5c1e…` with the uploader's claims |

### Checks before `202`

Every check runs before the answer, so the service never accepts a building it would refuse later.

| Rule | Error |
|---|---|
| `position` has finite `x`, `y`, `z` | `400` |
| `dimensions` has finite `width`, `height`, `depth`, all above 0 | `400` |
| `capacity` present, finite, 0 or more | `400 capacity must be a non-negative number` |
| `domains` not empty | `400 A building needs at least one domain` |
| Editor in **every** domain | `403`. One foreign domain refuses the whole upload |

Names never fail: a blank building name becomes the id, a blank room name the room id. The **client** chooses room ids; the **service** chooses the building id.

### The job after the worker

The worker does not mark the job done. It stays `pending`, its lease running, until `telemetry` answers.

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        A["provision_next: claim a job"] --> N{"job found?"}
        N -- no --> Sleep["sleep 50 ms"]
        N -- yes --> Pr["provision: upsert, publish, init columns"]
        Pr --> Ok{"upsert and publish worked?"}
        Ok -- no --> Fail["fail(id, error)"]
        Ok -- yes --> Wait["job stays pending, lease 30 s"]
        Wait --> Ans{"telemetry answers within 30 s?"}
        Ans -- yes --> Res["resolve"]
        Ans -- no --> Again["lease runs out, the worker claims it again and republishes"]
        Again --> Pr

**Example.** `telemetry` is down for 2 minutes. The job is claimed at 0 s, 30 s, 60 s, 90 s, 120 s: 5 attempts, 5 identical requests on Kafka. When `telemetry` comes back it registers the building 5 times, each an upsert, and answers 5 times. The first `ready` resolves the job; the other 4 match nothing.

- `init_preferences` only logs a failure. Its answer code is not checked.
- `attempts` grows but nothing reads it: there is no retry limit.

### Telemetry's answer

`adapters/driving/kafka_consumer.rs`, group `building-registrations-completed`, from the oldest unread message, commit by hand:

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant K as Kafka
        participant C as handle_payload
        participant P as Provisioning
        participant Q as MongoUploadQueue
        participant N as notification
        participant S as MongoBuildings
        K->>C: RegistrationCompleted
        C->>C: failure()
        C->>P: resolve(id, error)
        alt ready
            P->>Q: mark_ready: only if still pending
        else failed
            P->>Q: mark_failed: only if still pending
            P->>N: POST /trigger, as system:digital-twin
            P->>S: delete(id)
        end
        C->>K: commit offset

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        M["message"] --> Pa{"parses?"}
        Pa -- no --> Log1["log, commit: reading it again gives the same answer"]
        Pa -- yes --> R["resolve"]
        R --> O{"result"}
        O -- "Ok, job changed" --> T["record time since accept, commit"]
        O -- "Ok, nothing changed" --> C2["commit: a repeat"]
        O -- "Internal error" --> Re["stop, reconnect after 5 s, read the same message again"]
        O -- "other error" --> Log2["log, commit"]

**Why notify before delete.** `notification` finds the building's domains by calling back `GET /domain/{building}`. After the delete, that call would find nothing, and the alert would reach nobody.

**A repeat of `failed`.** `mark_failed` changes nothing, but the job is already `failed`: the delete may have failed last time, so only the delete runs again. No second alert.

**A `failed` for a building that is `ready`.** Logged, nothing deleted. So a `resync` that `telemetry` rejects can never delete a live twin.

### Polling the status

`GET /building/{id}/status` reads `pending_uploads`. `404 No upload with handle` when the job is unknown, or more than 1 h after it finished.

---

## Reading buildings

| Use case | Steps |
|---|---|
| `Buildings::get` | find by id or `404`; `can_read_building` or `403`; `normalize` |
| `Buildings::list_for_domain` | `is_member_of(domain)` or `403`; find by domain; `normalize` each |
| `Buildings::counts_per_domain` | more than 500 names: `400`; keep only readable domains; one MongoDB count per domain |

`normalize` repairs blank names on read: building name to its id, room name to its id. If anything changed, it writes the building back, so the repair happens once.

**Example.** `POST /buildings/counts {"domains":["eng","ops"]}` from a member of `eng` only. `ops` is dropped silently. Answer: `{"counts":{"eng":3}}`.

---

## Changing a building

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant B as Browser
        participant U as Buildings::update
        participant S as MongoBuildings
        participant T as telemetry
        B->>U: PATCH /building/b1 {name, domains, maxTemperature}
        U->>S: find, normalize
        U->>U: can_edit_domains(claims, current domains)
        U->>U: apply name and domains when present
        U->>S: upsert(building)
        U->>T: clone_thresholds: PUT /thresholds/buildings/b1 {name, rooms, maxTemperature?}
        T-->>U: 2xx
        U-->>B: Building

- Rooms cannot change: the patch has no room field.
- The call to `telemetry` carries the caller's own claims header.
- A non-2xx answer from `telemetry` fails the request with `500`, **after** step 5 already saved.

`POST /building/{id}/sync` reads the stored building and publishes the same request the worker would. Use it when `telemetry` lost a registration. It writes nothing here.

---

## Ownership lookup

`GET /domain/{building}` answers which domains own a building. Three services ask it:

| Caller | Why | Identity sent | Its own limits |
|---|---|---|---|
| `notification` | Who receives a breach alert | `system:notification-service`, no memberships | 2 s timeout, 15 min cache |
| `socket` | May this browser subscribe to this building? | The browser's claims | 2 s timeout, 60 s cache |
| `telemetry` | May this caller read this building's data? | The caller's claims | 5 s timeout, 60 s cache |

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        Q["domains_of(building)"] --> I{"a building with this id?"}
        I -- yes --> D1["its domains"]
        I -- no --> N["every building with this exact name"]
        N --> D2["all their domains, joined"]

The route checks only that a caller is signed in. It does **not** scope the answer to the caller: `system:` callers have no memberships and would always get `[]`.

**Example.** Two buildings named `Main Hall`, one in `eng`, one in `ops`. `GET /domain/Main%20Hall` answers `["eng","ops"]`. Asking by id answers only that building's domain.

---

## Placements

`PUT /building/{id}/placements`:

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant B as Browser
        participant H as save_placements
        participant C as PlacementChanges::checked
        participant P as Placements::apply
        participant S as MongoPlacements
        B->>H: {upsert: [{sensorId, position}], delete: [sensorId]}
        H->>H: position.to_coordinates for each upsert
        H->>C: checked(upsert, delete)
        C-->>H: PlacementChanges or 400
        H->>P: apply(b1, changes, claims)
        P->>P: building exists, can_edit_domains
        P->>S: one update_one with $set and $unset, upsert
        H-->>B: 204

`checked` is the only way to build `PlacementChanges`, so no layer can skip it:

| Rule | Why |
|---|---|
| At most 200 items in total | One request cannot hold the service up |
| Sensor id trimmed, not blank | |
| Only letters, digits, `-`, `_` | The id is a field name in MongoDB; `.` or `$` would be read as a path |
| Each sensor once, across `upsert` and `delete` | "Move and remove" in one batch has no clear meaning |
| Coordinates finite | |

**Example.** `{"upsert":[{"sensorId":"s1","position":{"x":4,"y":0.5,"z":6}}],"delete":["s2"]}` becomes one MongoDB update:

```json
{"$set": {"sensors.s1": {"x":4,"y":0.5,"z":6}}, "$unset": {"sensors.s2": ""}}
```

One document per building makes the batch all or nothing: this MongoDB runs alone, with no transactions, and one `update_one` is atomic. An empty batch writes nothing.

Only the position lives here. The sensor itself (type, room, name) belongs to `telemetry`; the sensor id is never checked against it.

---

## Estimating phones from Wi-Fi

### A tick, call by call

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant K as Kafka wifi-observations
        participant C as wifi_consumer.rs
        participant T as Tracking::observe
        participant G as Tracking::geometry
        participant Tk as Tracks::step
        participant O as KafkaEventProducer
        participant F as RedisDevicesFeed
        K->>C: tick, newest offset, auto-commit
        C->>C: handle_payload: parse WifiObservations
        C->>T: observe(tick, now)
        T->>T: problems() empty? tick under 30 s old? not older than the held one?
        T->>G: floor plan for this building
        G-->>T: Geometry, and whether a router moved
        T->>Tk: step(tick, geometry), tracks reset if a router moved
        Tk-->>T: dots
        T->>O: publish_occupancy, only if a router answered
        T->>F: publish(Devices), always, empty list included

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        In["tick"] --> P{"problems() empty?"}
        P -- no --> Inv["invalid, nothing published"]
        P -- yes --> A{"now minus tick over 30 s?"}
        A -- yes --> St["stale, nothing published"]
        A -- no --> H{"older than the building's held tick?"}
        H -- yes --> St
        H -- no --> Acc["accepted"]

Why read only the newest ticks: the collector polls about every 10 s, and Kafka keeps 10 minutes. Replayed after an outage, old ticks would move dots backwards.

### The floor plan

`Tracking::geometry` keeps one `Geometry` per building, reloaded when older than 30 s. A failed reload keeps the last good one: a database blip must not wipe every dot. If the placements changed, all tracks of the building start again.

`Geometry::of(building, placements)`:

1. **Floors.** Take each room's bottom, `y − height / 2`. Sort them; bottoms within 0.5 m are one floor.
2. **Cells.** On each floor, cover the rooms' outline with 0.5 m cells. Keep only cells inside a room.
3. **Routers.** Each placement goes on the floor its `y` falls on. Every placed sensor is used; only routers ever appear in a tick.
4. **Expected signal.** For every cell and every router on its floor: `−40 − 22 × log10(max(distance, 1)) − 5 × walls`. Walls are room outlines crossed by the straight line.

**Example, step 4.**

| Router | Distance | Walls | Expected |
|---|---|---|---|
| A | 3 m | 0 | −40 − 22 × 0.477 = **−50.5** dBm |
| B | 8 m | 1 | −40 − 22 × 0.903 − 5 = **−64.9** dBm |
| C | 12 m | 2 | −40 − 22 × 1.079 − 10 = **−73.7** dBm |

This is the same radio rule ap-simulator uses.

### Scoring the cells

`Geometry::posterior(floor, heard, prior)`:

1. Keep only routers placed on this floor.
2. For each cell, **misfit** = sum of (heard − expected)², divided by 2 × 4.5².
3. With a prior (where the phone was): drop cells farther than its reach, and add distance² / (2 × prior variance).
4. Weight of each cell = e^(lowest cost − its cost). The best cell weighs 1.
5. Position = weighted mean of cell centres; spread from the weighted distances, at least 0.5 m.

**Example.** A phone is heard at −51, −66, −75 dBm.

| Cell | Expected A, B, C | Misfit | Cost | Weight |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| X | −50.5, −64.9, −73.7 | 0.25 + 1.21 + 1.69 = 3.15 | 0.08 | 1 |
| Y | −60, −55, −70 | 81 + 121 + 25 = 227 | 5.60 | 0.004 |

Cell X wins by far.

`Geometry::fix` is the no-prior version for a lone burst. It picks the floor of the **loudest** router (a floor slab costs about 18 dB), and needs at least 3 routers placed there, or there is no fix.

### Following phones: `Tracks::step`

1. Group `connected` rows by token: each token's own signals.
2. For each burst:
   - its token is a connected phone: add its signals to that phone;
   - otherwise compute a `fix`, then find the phones that **fit**: one of the phone's own signals is within 9 dB of what that router should hear at the fix, and the phone could have walked there;
   - exactly one phone fits: add the burst to it;
   - none fits: someone passing, a dot with `connected: false`;
   - two or more fit: drop it. Guessing would give one phone the other's path.
3. Update each phone's track with `posterior`, using its last place as prior. Nothing in reach: start again from the signals alone.
4. Forget a token unseen for 3 ticks. One failed poll does not end a track.
5. Each track becomes a dot: floor, nearest room, `x`, `y` = floor height, `z`, `radius` = spread, `connected: true`.

**Prior example.** A track was last updated 10 s ago with variance 1 m².

| Value | Formula | Result |
|---|---|---|
| Variance now | 1 + (0.5 m/s × 10 s)² | 26 m² |
| Reach | 2 m/s × 10 s + 2 × √1 | 22 m |

Cells more than 22 m away are not even scored: nobody walks faster than 2 m/s.

### Room counts

When at least one router answered, `room_counts` sends `EstimatedOccupancy`: every room on a floor with a placed router starts at 0, then each **connected** dot adds 1 to its room. Passing dots do not count.

| Output | Where | Limit |
|---|---|---|
| Dots | Redis `twin:devices:{id}` | Gives up after 2 s; counted as `timeout` |
| Room counts | Kafka `estimated-occupancy`, key building, kept 1 h | Gives up after 2 s |

Both limits keep the Wi-Fi consumer from stalling: the next tick comes 10 s later anyway.

---

## Access rules

`service/authz.rs` runs Cedar in process, with the shared bundle from `backend/libs/auth-policy`, embedded at build time.

How a caller becomes a Cedar entity:

1. For each membership, look up the role's weight. An unknown role is skipped, never a wildcard.
2. Put the domain in every set the weight reaches: `domainsAsStandardCustomer`, `domainsAsBusinessStaff`, `domainsAsBusinessAdmin`, `domainsAsAdmin`.
3. The resource is the domain asked about.

| Helper | Cedar action | True when |
|---|---|---|
| `is_member_of(d)` | `Read` | Any known role in `d` |
| `can_read_building(ds)` | `Read` | Member of one of `ds`, or caller `system:ap-simulator` |
| `can_edit_domains(ds)` | `Edit` | `business_staff` or higher in **one** of `ds` |
| `can_edit_every(ds)` | `Edit` | `ds` not empty and editor in **all** |
| `filter_readable_domains_from_memberships` | `Read` | Keeps the readable ones |

**Example.** Ada is `admin` in `other`. Building in `eng` and `ops`. `can_read_building` is false: a platform admin is not a member of every domain.

---

## Failure handling

| What fails | Effect |
|---|---|
| MongoDB on a request | `500`, cause logged |
| MongoDB in the worker | Loop logs, sleeps 50 ms, tries again |
| Kafka publish in `provision` | `fail`: job `failed`, alert, twin deleted |
| `telemetry` silent | Job republished every 30 s |
| Store error while resolving | Answer read again after 5 s |
| `dashboard` init | Logged only |
| `notification` alert | Logged only |
| `telemetry` on `PATCH` | `500`, building already saved |
| Redis or Kafka on a Wi-Fi tick | Dropped after 2 s, counted |
| A consumer's stream breaks | Reconnect after 5 s |

---

## Metrics

| Name | Labels | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `http_requests_total`, `http_error_requests_total`, `http_request_duration_seconds` | method, route, status | Every route, by route template |
| `twin_provisioning_duration_seconds` | `ready`, `failed` | From accept to `telemetry`'s answer |
| `twin_wifi_ticks_total` | `accepted`, `invalid`, `stale` | Ticks read |
| `twin_wifi_tick_lag_seconds` | | From the collector's snapshot to reading it |
| `twin_devices_published_total` | `ok`, `error`, `timeout` | Dot lists to Redis |
| `twin_occupancy_published_total` | `ok`, `error`, `timeout` | Room counts to Kafka |

